# Project Rules for Claude in Cursor

## Git Discipline
- Never work directly on `main` or `master`
- Always create a new branch before making changes: `experiment/[short-description]`
- Run `git status` before starting any task — commit or stash pending changes first
- Confirm active branch before touching any files
- Use `git revert` to undo, never `git reset --hard`
- Commit frequently with clear messages: `git commit -m "feat: add user validation"`

## Parallel Agent Safety
- Before touching any file, state which files you intend to modify
- Never modify files outside the explicitly agreed scope
- If a required change is outside scope, stop and ask rather than proceeding
- One agent per file — never edit a file another agent owns in this session

## Code Changes
- Make the smallest change that solves the problem
- Don't refactor code outside the scope of the current task
- Never delete code — comment it out and explain why, let the user decide
- Always explain what you're about to do before doing it
- If a change affects more than 3 files, pause and confirm with the user first

## Architecture
- Respect existing patterns and conventions in the codebase — don't introduce new ones without asking
- Keep files small and single-responsibility
- Changes to shared interfaces or types must be flagged explicitly before proceeding

## Before Starting Any Task
1. Confirm the branch
2. State the files you'll touch
3. Summarize the approach in one sentence
4. Get implicit or explicit confirmation, then proceed

## When Uncertain
- Stop and ask rather than guess
- Prefer the simpler solution when two approaches seem equal
- If something feels risky, say so before doing it

---

## Project-Specific: District Heating Forecaster

### Model & Data Conventions
- The production inference path is always `get_mh_forecast_window()` — 48 direct LightGBM models
- The single-model fallback (`get_forecast_window`) is legacy code; do not extend it
- Feature prefix convention: lag/roll features at snapshot time `t` have no prefix; features at target time `t+h` use the `fc_` prefix
- `MH_FEATURES = MH_LAG_COLS + [f"fc_{c}" for c in MH_WEATHER_COLS + MH_CALENDAR_COLS]` — always 30 features; never add features without retraining all 48 models
- Training split: 2020–2023 train, 2024 test — never train on 2024 data

### Caching
- `init()` in `app.py` is decorated with `@st.cache_resource`; it runs once per server start
- After retraining models, the Streamlit server must be **fully restarted** — hot-reload does not flush `@st.cache_resource`
- When changing `MH_FEATURES`, always retrain before restarting the dashboard; a feature-count mismatch causes a `LightGBMError` at inference time

### Streamlit / Plotly Conventions
- Charts use Plotly with two subplots (demand + weather); the shared x-axis sits at the **top** of subplot 1 only
- `subplot_titles` must be omitted when using a top x-axis — they overlap with tick labels
- `showticklabels=True` must be set explicitly on the row-1 x-axis to override the `shared_xaxes` suppression
- Day-band annotations are drawn above the axis at `y=1.13` using `fig.add_annotation`
- Hover values are rounded to whole numbers via `hovertemplate`
- All colours come from the Gradyent-inspired palette; do not introduce new hex colours without discussion
- Legends are placed outside the chart area on the right (`legend` for demand, `legend2` for weather)

### Weather API (Open-Meteo)
- Current/future dates → `/v1/forecast`; historical/time-travel dates → `/v1/historical-forecast`
- Derived features must be recomputed from raw API output: `heating_degrees`, `temp_change_3h`, `wind_sin`, `wind_cos`
- The API call is optional (sidebar checkbox); always provide a graceful fallback to oracle weather from the CSV

### Git History Reference
- Zenodo dataset: https://zenodo.org/records/17177421
- GitHub repo: https://github.com/herbvibe/district-heating-forecaster
- Key commits to know:
  - `7ba6fa9` — added short-term lags (2h/3h/6h/12h) to all MH models
  - `88d3cd2` — real precipitation (rain+snow) from Open-Meteo
  - `8578cd6` — updated meta.json after retraining with 30-feature models
